        {
         "chapter_id": "HISTORY",
         "title": null,
         "paragraphs": [
          "The growth of the Ottoman Empire in the 15th century also saw the expansion of its economy, reflected in the construction of bedestens, or covered bazaars. Originally purposed for the sale of textiles, these indoor markets provided safe storage for valuable goods overnight, and they soon expanded their wares to include a wide variety of luxuries.",
          "As the empire’s capital, Istanbul acquired two bedestens at its heart in the decades following the city’s capture from the Byzantines. Built next to one another, the Iç Bedesten and the Sandal Bedesten were soon surrounded by outdoor markets, forming one large commercial space – the Grand Bazaar. Sitting at the focal point of many trade routes, the bazaar boasted a remarkable and uniquely diverse array of global goods across thousands of shops, overshadowing the markets of Europe and drawing visitors from around the globe.",
          "The Grand Bazaar remains an active market, and is one of the world’s most visited attractions."
         ]
        }
